**CAP Theorem** (also known as **Brewer's Theorem**) is a fundamental concept in distributed systems that states:

> In any distributed data system, **you can only achieve two out of the following three guarantees** at the same time:

### 1. **Consistency (C)**

Every read receives the most recent write or an error.

> ✅ All nodes return the same, up-to-date data.

### 2. **Availability (A)**

Every request receives a (non-error) response — without a guarantee that it contains the most recent write.

> ✅ System remains responsive.

### 3. **Partition Tolerance (P)**

The system continues to operate despite arbitrary partitioning due to network failures.

> ✅ The system doesn’t crash even if communication between some nodes is lost.

---

### The Trade-off:

A distributed system **must tolerate partitions (P)**. So, it can only choose between:

* **CP (Consistency + Partition Tolerance)** → Sacrifices Availability

  * Example: MongoDB (with strong consistency settings)
* **AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance)** → Sacrifices Consistency

  * Example: Couchbase, Cassandra
* **CA (Consistency + Availability)** → Not possible in practice when partitions happen

---

### Summary Table:

| Model | What It Guarantees                | Sacrifices                            | Example                             |
| ----- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| CP    | Consistency, Partition Tolerance  | Availability                          | HBase, MongoDB (in strict mode)     |
| AP    | Availability, Partition Tolerance | Consistency                           | Cassandra, DynamoDB                 |
| CA    | Consistency, Availability         | Partition Tolerance *(theoretically)* | Not feasible in real-world networks |

